WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.084
Secrets management is a core part of
Next.js security.

2
00:00:04.164 --> 00:00:08.682
API keys, database credentials, signing
secrets, session secrets,

3
00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:13.981
OAuth credentials, and service tokens
should remain server-side and come from

4
00:00:14.061 --> 00:00:17.801
protected configuration or secret
management systems.

5
00:00:17.881 --> 00:00:23.256
Public configuration and server-only
configuration must be clearly separated.

6
00:00:23.336 --> 00:00:28.010
A value that reaches a client bundle
should be assumed visible to users.

7
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:31.985
Secrets should not be committed, logged,
placed in images,

8
00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:36.116
exposed through previews, or copied into
support artifacts.

9
00:00:36.196 --> 00:00:41.104
Dependency hygiene matters because web
applications depend on packages,

10
00:00:41.184 --> 00:00:45.547
build tools, plugins, middleware, and
deployment integrations.

11
00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:50.457
Lockfiles, dependency review, update
discipline, package provenance,

12
00:00:50.537 --> 00:00:53.575
and security advisories all help manage
risk.

13
00:00:53.655 --> 00:00:57.083
Scanning is useful, but it is not the
whole practice.

14
00:00:57.163 --> 00:01:02.460
Teams should pay particular attention to
packages that handle authentication,

15
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.214
parsing, rendering, serialization, file
processing, data fetching,

16
00:01:07.294 --> 00:01:12.670
or build-time behavior because those
packages sit close to security boundaries.

17
00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:17.190
Safe logging supports investigation
without becoming a data leak.

18
00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:22.178
Logs may need route names, request IDs,
user or service identity, outcome,

19
00:01:22.258 --> 00:01:27.633
timing, authorization denials, validation
failures, and high-impact actions.

20
00:01:27.713 --> 00:01:32.076
They should avoid secrets, credentials,
tokens, session values,

21
00:01:32.156 --> 00:01:35.506
private payloads, and unnecessary personal
data.

22
00:01:35.586 --> 00:01:40.182
Redaction and retention rules should be
designed before an incident,

23
00:01:40.262 --> 00:01:45.559
not improvised after sensitive data has
already been copied across log systems.

24
00:01:45.639 --> 00:01:47.976
Deployment review closes the loop.

25
00:01:48.056 --> 00:01:52.575
Production and development settings should
differ intentionally.

26
00:01:52.655 --> 00:01:56.939
Debug routes, stack traces, development
middleware, test data,

27
00:01:57.019 --> 00:02:02.317
preview environments, staging credentials,
CORS settings, security headers,

28
00:02:02.397 --> 00:02:07.694
reverse proxy behavior, and environment
variables should all be part of release

29
00:02:07.774 --> 00:02:08.320
review.

30
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.242
Monitoring and incident response readiness
matter because Next.js security continues

31
00:02:14.322 --> 00:02:15.725
after code is merged.

32
00:02:15.805 --> 00:02:20.557
Teams need to know when traffic, errors,
denials, dependency behavior,

33
00:02:20.637 --> 00:02:22.740
or data access patterns change.
